response_a:
Below is one way to fix your code. The issues were that:

1. You defined a loadAndModifySVG() function to perform the text replacement but then never used it (instead, you did a plain fetch that simply loads the SVG without modifying it).
2. The replacement string used nested quotes that were not escaped properly.

Try the following updated version of fetch_svg.js:

-------------------------------------------------
"use strict";

// Function to load and modify SVG content
function loadAndModifySVG(url, callback) {
  fetch(url)
    .then(response => {
      if (!response.ok) {
        throw new Error("Network response was not ok");
      }
      return response.text();
    })
    .then(svgText => {
      // Replace the <svg version="1.0" with one that includes the onload attribute
      // Note: Escape inner quotes with a backslash to avoid breaking the string.
      const modifiedSVG = svgText.replace(
        /<svg version="1\.0"/,
        '<svg onload="createAlarmSVGLinks(\'../wtrtrmt_alarms.htm\')" version="1.1"'
      );
      callback(modifiedSVG);
    })
    .catch(error => console.error("Error loading SVG:", error));
}

// Function to insert the modified SVG into the container div
function insertSVGIntoDiv(svgContent) {
  const svgContainer = document.getElementById("svgContainer");
  svgContainer.innerHTML = svgContent;
}

// When the DOM is loaded, fetch and insert the modified SVG
document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", () => {
  loadAndModifySVG("../images/wtrtrmt42_01_esmx.svg", insertSVGIntoDiv);
});
-------------------------------------------------

A few notes:

• In the replacement, the regular expression uses 1\.0 (with an escaped dot) so it matches precisely "1.0".  
• The onload attribute’s inner quotes around ../wtrtrmt_alarms.htm are escaped with backslashes.  
• We now call loadAndModifySVG() during the "DOMContentLoaded" event so that the modified SVG (with the onload attribute included) gets inserted into the div.

Make sure that your HTML file includes a container with the id "svgContainer" and that createAlarmSVGLinks() is defined (or will be defined) elsewhere. For example, your HTML body might look like this:

-------------------------------------------------
<div class="box2">
  <div id="svgContainer"></div>
</div>
<script src="js/fetch_svg.js"></script>
-------------------------------------------------

This updated approach should load the SVG, perform the replacement, and make it visible in your browser.

