Below is one common culprit and some suggestions on how to fix your code.

──────────────────────────────
Problem: Using the Transform as a “start marker”
──────────────────────────────
You’re currently doing this in moveToCenter:
  startMarker = this.transform;
This means that startMarker isn’t a snapshot of the starting position—it’s a reference to the same Transform that’s moving. As your card’s transform moves, startMarker.position updates too. That explains why your interpolation seems off; the “start” position isn’t fixed.

──────────────────────────────
Fix: Store the Starting Position in a Vector3
──────────────────────────────
Instead of storing a reference to the Transform, you can capture the starting position in a Vector3. For example:
  Vector3 startPos = transform.position;
Then, when Lerp’ing you use this startPos instead of startMarker.position. This ensures that endPos remains interpolated from the original position rather than a moving one.

──────────────────────────────
Other Suggestions and Adjustments
──────────────────────────────
1. Calculate journeyAmount after updating the elapsed time.
  Currently you are doing:
    journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;
    if (journeyAmount < 1f) { distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed; … }
  It’s better to update the distance first and then compute the ratio like this:
    distance = (Time.time - startTime) * speed;
    journeyAmount = distance / journeyLength;

2. Coroutine Stopping
  Calling StopCoroutine(move()) inside stopAnimation isn’t ideal because move() is a method that returns an enumerator and you’re calling StartCoroutine(move()) without saving the reference. It’s better to do one of these:
   • Use a boolean flag to exit the while loop or let the while loop finish when journeyAmount >= 1
   • Save the Coroutine handle when you call StartCoroutine, and then stop using that handle if needed

──────────────────────────────
Revised Code Example
──────────────────────────────
Below is your code revised with these suggestions:

